  "result": "At closing time, a library changes its voice. During the day, it hums with questions: keyboards clicking, pages turning, chairs dragging across the floor. But after the final patron leaves, the building exhales. The shelves stand like patient streets, lined with doors into other lives.\n\nI like to imagine that books continue speaking when nobody is listening. Not aloud, exactly. Their conversations might be made of proximity. A cookbook beside a history of migration exchanges memories of spice routes. A field guide to birds leans toward a collection of poems, offering feathers, weather, and the shape of flight. On the lowest shelf, a battered adventure novel reassures a travel memoir that getting lost is sometimes the beginning of the story.\n\nLibraries are often praised as storehouses of knowledge, but that description feels too still. A library is more like a garden whose seeds wait for attention. Reading is the weather. Each visitor brings a different season, and every book blooms differently in a different mind.\n\nPerhaps that is why old notes found in margins feel so intimate. A stranger underlined one sentence fifty years ago, and suddenly time folds. Two people who will never meet pause at the same words. The mark is small, but it says: I was here. This mattered to me.\n\nWhen morning returns, the doors open, and the quiet city of shelves fills again. Someone arrives seeking a fact, someone else shelter, another person merely a warm place to sit. The library makes room for all.",
